# Service layer

## Real structure

Service contracts live in `service-contracts`, and implementations live in
`services`.

```java
// service-contracts/src/.../ClassUserService.java
public interface ClassUserService {
    ClassUser createClassUser(String email, String password, String username);
    Optional<ClassUser> findClassUserByEmail(String email);
    Optional<ClassUser> findClassUserById(long id);
}
```

```java
// services/src/.../ClassUserServiceImpl.java
@Service
public class ClassUserServiceImpl implements ClassUserService {
    private final ClassUserDao classUserDao;

    @Autowired
    public ClassUserServiceImpl(final ClassUserDao classUserDao) {
        this.classUserDao = classUserDao;
    }
}
```

Do not assume an `interfaces/` module. In this repository, contracts live in
`*-contracts` modules.

## Responsibilities

- Contain business logic for the middle layer.
- Orchestrate access to persistence DAO contracts.
- Expose domain operations to `webapp`.

## Rules

- Inject DAO interfaces from `persistence-contracts`.
- Do not use `JdbcTemplate` or SQL inside `services`.
- Do not return web-layer objects.
- Do not build `ModelAndView` or access request/session state.
- Prefer constructor injection.

## Testing

`services` tests use JUnit 5 + Mockito. Real example:

- `ClassUserServiceImplTest`
- `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)`
- `@Mock ClassUserDao`
- `@InjectMocks ClassUserServiceImpl`

This matches the repository-wide testing split:

- `services`: Mockito for unit tests.
- `persistence`: HSQLDB for integration tests.
- runtime: PostgreSQL.
